# THE ENCRYPTING FRONT DOOR for the hosted machine.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: the sign-in service and the character server both speak plain, readable text. On your own
# desk that is harmless. Over the internet it means a player's password and their save travel where anyone on
# the path can read them. This program stands in front of both, holds the certificate, and is the only thing
# the internet can reach.
#
# WHY CADDY AND NOT SOMETHING ELSE: it fetches and renews the certificate itself, with no second program and
# nothing to remember. The whole configuration is the twenty lines below.
#
# WHAT YOU NEED FIRST:
#   1. A domain name. A certificate cannot be issued for a bare numeric address, so this is not optional.
#   2. Three DNS "A" records, all pointing at the machine's address:
#        accounts.example.com    -> 203.0.113.10
#        characters.example.com  -> 203.0.113.10
#        patch.example.com       -> 203.0.113.10     (the launcher's update folder)
#      A name per service rather than one because the two services are two different programs on two ports, and a
#      name each means neither the game nor these services need to learn about paths they did not have before.
#   3. Ports 80 and 443 open on the machine. Port 80 is how the certificate is issued and renewed; closing it
#      makes renewal fail silently three months later.
#   4. Both services started with BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1, so the only way in is through this file.
#
# NOTHING IS TYPED IN THIS FILE. Every name, address, port and folder below is read from settings.env — the one
# file every program here reads — through {$NAME}: PUBLIC_ACCOUNTS_URL, PUBLIC_CHAR_SERVER_URL and PATCH_URL are
# the three web addresses (written with their https://), CERT_EMAIL, PATCH_DIR, and the two ports.
#
# INSTALL AND RUN (Debian/Ubuntu), with the project at /srv/unwritten:
#   sudo apt install -y caddy
#   sudo cp deploy/Caddyfile /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#   sudo systemctl edit caddy        # add these two lines, so Caddy reads settings.env:
#                                    #   [Service]
#                                    #   EnvironmentFile=/srv/unwritten/settings.env
#   sudo systemctl restart caddy
#
# PROVE IT WORKS:
#   curl -s https://accounts.example.com/health        # want {"ok":true}
#   curl -s http://203.0.113.10:8080/health            # want NOTHING — the plain door must be shut

{
	# Where the certificate authority writes to you when a renewal is failing. A real address you read.
	email {$CERT_EMAIL}
}

{$PUBLIC_ACCOUNTS_URL} {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:{$PORT}
}

{$PUBLIC_CHAR_SERVER_URL} {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:{$CHAR_SERVER_PORT}
}

# THE UPDATE FOLDER the launcher downloads from — tools/publish-update.py writes it; copy it here. Plain files,
# served as they are. The character server reads its version from the SAME folder (PATCH_DIR), so the version it
# demands is the one this folder hands out.
{$PATCH_URL} {
	root * {$PATCH_DIR}
	file_server
}

# THE WEBSITE is served by the block above: publishing writes it into the update folder beside the updates. Its
# Server Status page reads the character server from a browser, and a browser refuses that unless the character
# server allows this exact address — including the https:// and with no trailing slash. It allows PATCH_URL's
# address by default; served from any other address, set it in settings.env:
#     WIKI_ORIGINS=https://play.example.com
# The refusal is SILENT, with nothing in the browser's console, so a status page that says it cannot reach the
# character server while the server answers is almost always this.

# DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: the world servers. Players reach those on a different kind of connection that
# certificates do not cover, and its protection is the one-time entry ticket the character server hands out.
# Putting them behind this would break them, not secure them.
